CAT = "rsracSafety"; SEG = "SAF"
Q = [
("A large refrigerant leak fills a basement machine room. What is the main hazard to a worker entering it?",
 "Lack of oxygen", ["Burns from hot gas", "Ozone build-up", "Static discharge"],
 "Most refrigerant vapours are heavier than air and collect low, pushing out oxygen. A worker can be overcome without warning, which is why machine rooms have refrigerant detectors and ventilation."),
("Halocarbon refrigerant touches a glowing heater element. Which harmful products can form?",
 "Hydrogen fluoride", ["Carbon dioxide and water", "Nitrogen and argon", "Methane gas"],
 "Hot surfaces and flames break fluorinated refrigerants down into hydrogen fluoride and other acids, which are toxic to breathe. Ventilate and clear the leak before running burners or brazing."),
("Before replacing a condenser fan motor on a rooftop unit, what isolates it safely?",
 "Your own lock on the disconnect", ["Thermostat set to off", "Unit switched off at the control board", "Fan breaker turned off by the tenant"],
 "Lockout means each worker applies a personal lock and tag at the energy-isolating device and verifies zero energy. A thermostat or control switch can be turned back on by someone else."),
("After locking out a compressor circuit, what should you do before touching the terminals?",
 "Test for voltage with a proven meter", ["Wait five minutes for it to cool", "Remove the tag to check the label", "Jump the contactor to drain it"],
 "Verify the circuit is dead with a meter checked on a known live source before and after. Run capacitors can also hold a charge and are discharged before handling."),
("Liquid refrigerant sprays onto a worker's bare hand. What is the first aid for the cold burn?",
 "Lukewarm water, no rubbing", ["Rub it with snow", "Hot water from the tap", "Rub it briskly to warm it"],
 "Refrigerant safety data sheets direct gentle rewarming with lukewarm water and medical attention. Rubbing damages frozen tissue and hot water can burn skin that has lost feeling."),
("What eye protection suits connecting hoses to a pressurized refrigerant system?",
 "Side-shield safety glasses", ["Tinted welding goggles", "Prescription glasses alone", "Face shield over bare eyes"],
 "A hose or valve core can release liquid refrigerant toward the face, so side-shielded safety glasses are the minimum. Prescription lenses without side shields leave gaps."),
("Why does brazing near a wood-framed wall call for a fire watch after the work stops?",
 "Hidden material can smoulder", ["Brass fittings stay radioactive", "Nitrogen can ignite later", "Flux turns into a fuel gas"],
 "Heat travels into hidden spaces and insulation can smoulder long after the torch is off. Hot-work rules require someone to watch the area for a set time after brazing."),
("A small A2L refrigerant leak is suspected in a mechanical room. Which precaution comes first?",
 "Ventilate and remove sparks", ["Run a space heater to dry it", "Pressurize the room with oxygen", "Braze the leak at once"],
 "A2L refrigerants have low flammability, so a concentrated leak near an ignition source can burn. Ventilate, remove sparks and flames, and use A2L-rated tools."),
("Why should a refrigerant cylinder never be stored in a hot vehicle cab?",
 "Pressure rises with temperature", ["The oil inside freezes", "The valve seat swells shut", "The refrigerant turns to a solid"],
 "Cylinder pressure follows the refrigerant's saturation temperature, so heat raises it toward the relief setting. Store cylinders upright, secured and away from heat."),
("Which ladder setup is correct when climbing onto a flat roof to reach a condensing unit?",
 "Secured, 1 m past the roof edge", ["Leaned on the eavestrough unsecured", "Set at a 2 to 1 angle", "Footed by a pail of tools"],
 "An extension ladder used for access extends about 1 m past the landing and is secured against slipping, set at about 4 to 1. A pail of tools at its foot does not secure it."),
("A new heat pump model uses a refrigerant you have never handled. What is the best first step?",
 "Read the service literature", ["Treat it the same as R-22", "Charge it by the sight glass", "Ask the homeowner how it works"],
 "Continuous learning in the trade includes reading installation and service instructions for new equipment and refrigerants, since pressures, oils and safety classes differ."),
("Why do mechanics take manufacturer training on electronically commutated motors and inverter drives?",
 "Diagnosis differs from older equipment", ["It replaces the trade licence", "It is needed to use a meter", "It removes the need for lockout"],
 "Variable-speed motors and drives use electronic controls and communicating signals, so the test methods differ from simple single-speed motors. Training keeps diagnosis accurate."),
("An apprentice keeps overcharging small systems. What mentoring approach works best?",
 "Demonstrate, then watch them try", ["Charge every system yourself", "Hand them the manual and leave", "Point out the error to the client"],
 "Effective mentoring shows the skill, lets the learner practise it with feedback, and checks understanding. Doing the job for them teaches nothing."),
("A building operator asks why a chiller was shut down. How should you communicate the reason?",
 "In plain terms, with the next steps", ["Only in technical shorthand", "Through the invoice later", "By leaving a note on the unit"],
 "Clear communication with clients covers what was found, why it matters and what happens next, in words they understand. A note or invoice alone leaves them guessing."),
("Two trades need the same ceiling space for ductwork and refrigerant piping. Who should the conflict go to?",
 "Site supervisor", ["Whoever arrives first", "Building occupants", "Refrigerant supplier"],
 "Space conflicts between trades are resolved through the site supervisor or coordination meeting, so the layout follows the drawings and each trade's requirements."),
("Why is a respirator with the right cartridges needed when cleaning a coil with chemical cleaner?",
 "Cleaner mist can harm the lungs", ["It keeps refrigerant in the coil", "It stops the coil from icing", "It protects the fins from corrosion"],
 "Acid and alkaline coil cleaners give off mists that irritate the airways. The cleaner's safety data sheet names the PPE, including respiratory protection when needed."),
]
